    "explanation": "To the rider and to the driver: the AI cannot decide this case, so it goes to a human reviewer. The 5.00 SGD fee is held — not collected from the rider and not paid to the driver — until that review.\n\nWhat the record shows. The driver accepted the trip at 20:40:22 and arrived at Block 115 at 20:46:45; the driver-device GPS trace corroborates the arrival, its nearest fix 2.6 m from the pickup point, far inside the 50 m STANDARD arrival radius fixed by POL-NS-21, all fixes passing the 25 m accuracy filter. The trip was on-demand with no scheduled time, so the 300-second free wait ran from arrival and expired at 20:51:45; the driver cancelled at 20:53:00, 75 seconds after expiry. Before cancelling the driver sent two in-app messages (20:47:00 and 20:50:20) and placed one in-app call (20:49:30, unanswered, 20 s). The payments ledger (EV-FARE-05066) shows a single cancellation-fee line of 5.00 SGD, total_charged 5.00, payment_status CHARGED, refund_issued_sgd 0.00 — exactly the POL-NS-22 fee. None of this is in dispute between the parties, and the rider's account of never receiving a message or call is explained by both messages being delivered but unread and the call going unanswered.\n\nWhy the AI cannot rule. The driver's profile carries fraud_flags = 1 (EV-DPROF-05066). Under POL-GEN-22 and POL-NS-26 that raises the confidence this ruling must reach to 0.95, whatever the 5.00 SGD value of the dispute. The half of the POL-NS-23 contact obligation that requires the driver to have messaged the rider is carried by the chat log EV-CHAT-05066, which is impaired: both messages were delivered and never read, issue code UNREAD_MESSAGE, weight_multiplier 0.85 (POL-GEN-02). The trip record does not independently carry the driver's message-sending — the message count it holds is a summary of the same chat events, so the two records are one carrier — and the cap on confidence is therefore 0.85. That is below the 0.95 the fraud flag requires, so no AI ruling upholding or voiding the fee can be issued. The charge is held rather than collected or refunded.\n\nThe parties' arguments. Rider: your point that the driver's fraud flag lifts the required confidence to 0.95 is accepted and applied; your point that the impaired chat log caps confidence at 0.85 is accepted and is what decides the case. The ledger does show you carrying 5.00 SGD with nothing refunded, but escalation holds that charge rather than returning it. Driver: your points on arrival and on the expired free wait are accepted and are not in doubt, and the contact was in fact attempted — two messages and a call, all before cancellation. Your claim that the trip record independently carries the message-sending at weight 1.0 is rejected: the count in the trip record is derived from the same chat events as the chat log, so they are one carrier at 0.85. Your POL-NS-24 point is accepted — the driver arrived and attempted contact, so neither the refund nor the goodwill credit in that clause is engaged. Your point that no party's profile can decide this trip is accepted; the rider's 'he does this to collect fees' is an unsupported pattern allegation and the telemetry, not the profiles, is what speaks to this trip.\n\nMissing artifact. The traffic advisory EV-ADVIS-05066-01 is MISSING, weight 0.0, and carries no fact (POL-GEN-02(3)); nothing in POL-NS-21 makes traffic relevant, since the free wait runs a fixed 300 s from arrival, so its absence opens no question.\n\nReferrals. None. The fee charged is exactly the 5.00 SGD POL-NS-22 sets, so no fee-amount referral arises (POL-NS-22); the rider's profile carries no no_show_cancellations_30d counter, so the POL-NS-25 repeat no-show flag does not fire; and there was no driver no-show, so no POL-NS-24 goodwill credit.\n\nOpen question for the reviewer. Whether the driver's contact actually reached the rider — both messages sat unread and the call rang unanswered — and whether, on the intact and impaired evidence taken together, the POL-NS-23 obligation and the 5.00 SGD fee should stand."
